 SKELETON-GROUPED COLUMNAR coding (bigoracle §4.2) -- the last untested structural axis.
 Conservatively tokenize each distinct line into a SKELETON (punct/keyword/whitespace literal
 fragments) + SLOTS ([A-Za-z0-9_] runs); group by identical skeleton; ship skeleton once + slot
 tokens interned + token-placement stream (columnar or row-wise). Reproduce: --skeleton.

 VERDICT: does NOT break the entropy floor. It TIES front-code at z3 and LOSES to it at z19,
 because the decomposition discards the cross-line byte-LZ structure front-code+z19 exploits.
 The token-PLACEMENT stream (which of N tokens fills each slot) is the irreducible core (~4-5 MiB,
 ~68% of the total) -- genuine content entropy, not hidden structure. So DuckDB's distinct-line
 content is entropy-capped at ~260x (z3) / ~375x (z19, offline). 400x is NOT reachable on the
 DuckDB dict by ANY measured method: z3..z19, LDM, lexical front-coding, or skeleton-columnar.

 corpus   z3-plain  front-code_z3  skel-col-int_z3  skel-row-int_z3 | front-code_z19  skel-col-int_z19  skel-row-int_z19
 DuckDB    7.875      7.293          7.275            7.028         |   5.054           6.017             5.669
 LLVM      7.322      6.647          7.439            7.267         |   4.849           6.171             5.866
 (front-code is the best z3-speed dict transform on both; skeleton-columnar never wins.)
================================================================================

